Translations
============

meld-qt_<language>.ts are the program's translations, in Qt Linguist's
format: open them in Qt Linguist (linguist6, or "linguist" with Qt 6) to
translate. meld-qt_en.ts only has the plural forms of English messages
(e.g., "1 unpushed commit"), which messages with a number have in their
plural in the sources ("%n unpushed commits").

The translations are built into the program, and it uses the one for the
user's language (LANGUAGE, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES or LANG on Linux, as gettext
reads them, or the system's language), and Qt's own translations for its
standard dialogs and buttons. Messages without a translation are in English.
Translations that are unfinished in Qt Linguist aren't built in, as gettext
leaves out fuzzy ones.

Updating them from the sources
------------------------------

The .ts files have to be updated with lupdate when the sources' messages
change (new messages, or ones that have changed or gone). Building doesn't do
this (it only builds the .ts files into the program), with either build
system:

    cmake --build build --target update_translations
    make update_translations          # in a qmake build folder

In Qt Creator, this is a make step with the target update_translations (or
the CMake target), rather than Tools > External > Linguist > Update
Translations, which runs lupdate on the project without these options and
would read the tests too. Both targets run lupdate on src/, which gives the
same files; by hand, from the top folder:

    lupdate -I src src -locations relative -no-obsolete \
        -ts $(ls translations/meld-qt_*.ts | grep -v '_en\.ts$')
    lupdate -I src src -locations relative -no-obsolete -pluralonly \
        -ts translations/meld-qt_en.ts

The files only have the messages the sources have (-no-obsolete): when a
message changes, lupdate offers its old translation for the new one, as
unfinished, and drops the old one.

A message with a number takes it as %n, with tr(text, disambiguation, n),
rather than choosing its singular or plural in the code; its English forms
are then translated in meld-qt_en.ts. Comments for translators go just before
the message, as //: comments.

A new language
--------------

lupdate makes the file, from the top folder:

    lupdate -I src src -locations relative -no-obsolete -ts translations/meld-qt_<language>.ts

Then run CMake or qmake again: both build systems find the files here. The
language is as Qt names it (de, pt_BR, sr_Latn): a file for "de" is used for
any German, and one for "pt_BR" before "pt" in Brazil.

Preferences lists the language by its name in that language, as Qt gives it.
Where that doesn't tell it apart from another translation (pt and pt_BR, say),
add a name to nativeName() in src/core/translations.cpp; tst_translations
checks that every language has a name of its own.
